# Q1 keyword audit: the `ires` keyword (secondary entry point registration).
# A top-level `ires NAME;` registers NAME as a secondary entry; the
# registration is INERT in a default run — main stays the entry — which is
# exactly what this pin proves byte-identically on all three engines
# (VM / tree-walk / oracle): if registration ever leaked into default entry
# selection, this file's output would change.
# The `operon run --ires` flag leg (first registered name becomes the entry,
# note 'cap-independent entry via --ires') is honored by both Rust engines;
# the oracle CLI currently accepts the flag but ignores it (Q1-1 finding,
# vault REVIEWS.md) — a flag-leg pin joins when the oracle lane repairs it.
ires secondary;
gene secondary() {
    promote("secondary-ran")
}
gene main() {
    promote("main-ran")
}
